  "standard": "STAT.INF.A.1",
  "difficulty": 1,
  "prompt": "In a random sample of $100$ shoppers, $50$ say they prefer paper bags, so $\\hat{p} = 0.50$. What is the standard error of $\\hat{p}$, $\\sqrt{\\frac{\\hat{p}(1-\\hat{p})}{n}}$?",
  "answerType": "numeric",
  "primaryAnswer": "0.05",
  "alternativeAnswers": [],
  "acceptanceMode": "numeric-tolerance",
  "numericTolerance": 0.001,
  "hint": "Plug $\\hat{p} = 0.5$ and $n = 100$ into the formula.",
  "hints": [
    {
      "level": "nudge",
      "text": "Plug $\\hat{p} = 0.5$ and $n = 100$ into the formula."
    },
    {
      "level": "guide",
      "text": "$\\frac{0.5 \\times 0.5}{100} = 0.0025$. Now square-root."
    },
    {
      "level": "reveal",
      "text": "$\\sqrt{0.0025} = 0.05$."
    }
  ],
  "explanation": [
    "The standard error estimates how much $\\hat{p}$ varies from sample to sample, using $\\hat{p}$ in place of the unknown $p$.",
    "$\\hat{p}(1-\\hat{p})/n = (0.5)(0.5)/100 = 0.0025$.",
    "$SE = \\sqrt{0.0025} = 0.05$, or 5 percentage points."
  ],
  "alternativeExplanations": [
    {
      "title": "A handy benchmark",
      "steps": [
        "With $\\hat{p} = 0.5$, the SE is $0.5/\\sqrt{n}$.",
        "$\\sqrt{100} = 10$, so $SE = 0.5/10 = 0.05$.",
        "This is the largest SE possible for a given $n$; any other $\\hat{p}$ gives a smaller one."
      ]
    }
  ],
